<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Princely state</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as princely states</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A princely state was a nominally sovereign state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> ruled by an Indian prince under British paramountcy. Several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> dynasties, such as those of Gwalior, Indore and Baroda, became princely states. Accounts of the Maratha Empire note that Maratha administrative models influenced these states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/princely-state.jpg" alt="Princely state" width="320" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edinburgh Geographical Institute; J. G. Bartholomew and Sons. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_Indian_Empire_1909_Imperial_Gazetteer_of_India.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The new regime abandoned the Company’s policy of annexing <b>princely states</b> and sought instead to win the loyalty of landed and princely elites.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Hyderabad was the largest and most populous of the Indian <b>princely states</b>, covering some 214,000 square kilometres and home to more than sixteen million people at the census of 1941.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hyderabad-state">Hyderabad State · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>They left hill and sea forts, temples and ghats, as well as successor <b>princely states</b> such as Baroda, Gwalior and Indore that lasted until Indian independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dominion of India, led by Prime Minister Jawaharlal Nehru, integrated more than five hundred <b>princely states</b>, using force in Hyderabad in 1948, and fought a first war with Pakistan over Kashmir in 1947–1948.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When British rule ended in August 1947, Rajputana consisted of some two dozen <b>princely states</b> and chiefships, among them ancient Rajput kingdoms such as Mewar (Udaipur), Marwar (Jodhpur), Jaipur and Bikaner, together with the small British province of Ajmer-Merwara.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The rest belonged to more than five hundred <b>princely states</b>, whose rulers kept their thrones by accepting British paramountcy.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Background</a></li></ul></section>
